[('u(x)','Standard uncertainty of an input estimate x','same units as x'),('∂f/∂x','Sensitivity of a result to an input, holding others fixed','result units/input units'),('cov(x,y)','Shared variation between two input errors','units of x times units of y')],
[('Linearize the measurement model.','For small changes, δf ≈ Σ(∂f/∂xᵢ)δxᵢ. This is the first-order Taylor approximation: each input is weighted by its sensitivity.'),('Square and average.','If the input errors are uncorrelated, cross terms vanish and their variances add. A product or quotient is especially simple after taking a differential of its logarithm.'),('Apply the quotient to density.','For ρ = m/V, the derivatives are 1/V and −m/V². Dividing the resulting variance by ρ² gives the sum of squared relative standard uncertainties.')],
'u²(ρ) ≈ u²(m)/V² + m²u²(V)/V⁴\n[u(ρ)/ρ]² ≈ [u(m)/m]² + [u(V)/V]²',
'For m = 10.00 g with u(m) = 0.01 g and V = 5.00 mL with u(V) = 0.02 mL, ρ = 2.000 g mL⁻¹ and u(ρ) ≈ 0.00825 g mL⁻¹. A suitable uncertainty report is approximately (2.000 ± 0.008) g mL⁻¹, with the ± identified as standard uncertainty.',
'Add covariance terms for correlated inputs. Linear propagation can fail for large uncertainties, near-zero denominators, or strongly nonlinear models. A stated standard uncertainty is not a guaranteed error bound.',
