[('U; T','Internal energy; thermodynamic temperature','J; K'),('⟨E_trans⟩','Mean translational kinetic energy of one ideal-gas particle','J per particle'),('Phase; component','Homogeneous physical region; chemically independent constituent','concepts with distinct roles')],
[('Separate identity from arrangement.','An H₂O molecule remains H₂O through melting and boiling. The distribution of particle positions and energies changes, so physical properties can change without a change in molecular formula.'),('State the kinetic model precisely.','For a classical ideal gas at thermal equilibrium, mean translational kinetic energy is 3k_BT/2. Total internal energy can also include rotation, vibration, and interactions; the ideal-gas translational formula is not a general expression for every substance.'),('Use thermodynamics for phase choice.','At fixed T and p, the stable phase has lower molar Gibbs energy. At coexistence the chemical potential of a component is equal in its coexisting phases. Equal phase potentials do not mean equal density.')],
'Ideal-gas translation: ⟨E_trans⟩ = 3k_BT/2; phase coexistence: μ_solid = μ_liquid',
'At one atmosphere near the normal melting point, ice and water can coexist while their densities differ. Supplying energy changes the phase proportions until the transition completes.',
