[('Integrate a heat capacity within a phase.','At constant pressure with only pV work, dH = C_p dT. Integrate over the interval; if C_p is approximately constant, q_p ≈ C_pΔT = mc_pΔT.'),('Account for latent enthalpy separately.','At the coexistence temperature, energy can change the fractions of two phases without changing T. A complete transition of n moles requires nΔH_transition.'),('Connect the plateau to equilibrium.','For a reversible phase transition at T_tr, ΔG = ΔH − T_trΔS = 0, so ΔS_transition = ΔH_transition/T_tr. Coexistence, not absence of molecular motion, explains the plateau.')],
'q_total ≈ mc_sΔT_s + nΔH_fus + mc_lΔT_l + nΔH_vap + mc_gΔT_g\nAt coexistence: ΔS_transition = ΔH_transition/T_tr',
'For 1.00 mol of ice at 0 °C melting and then warming liquid water to 10 °C at about 1 atm: q ≈ 6.01 kJ + (18.02 g)(4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹)(10 K) ≈ 6.76 kJ.',
'Use the terms actually traversed, not all terms automatically. Assume fixed pressure, appropriate pure-substance transition data, negligible heat losses, and heat capacities valid over the chosen interval.',
[central('§11.4, heating curves',533),central('§19.2, entropy and reversible heat',910)],
