[('Connect oxyanion naming to proton transfer.','Sulfate and hydrogen sulfate are different species related by protonation. Their formulas and charges obey charge conservation, but the distribution in water depends on equilibrium, not just the compound’s original name.'),('Separate acid composition from acid strength.','HCl(aq) names hydrochloric acid in water, where proton transfer is extensive under ordinary dilute conditions. A hydrogen-containing formula alone does not make every H equally acidic. K_a describes the transfer tendency in a specified medium.'),('Derive a hydrate ratio by material balance.','If heating removes only water, mass lost divided by M(H₂O) gives moles of water. Divide by moles of remaining dry salt. The ratio identifies a stoichiometric hydrate if the solid reaches the intended composition.')],
'HA + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + A⁻; K_a = a(H₃O⁺)a(A⁻)/a(HA) with a(H₂O) ≈ 1\nx = [(m_hydrate − m_dry)/M(H₂O)]/[m_dry/M(salt)]',
'If 2.50 g of a hydrate leaves 1.60 g of anhydrous CuSO₄, using rounded molar masses 18.02 and 159.6 gives x ≈ (0.90/18.02)/(1.60/159.6) ≈ 5.0, consistent with the pentahydrate within the stated precision.',
