[('Start with particle counts.','For N_i atoms of isotope i, total mass is ΣN_i m_i. Divide by N_total and substitute f_i = N_i/N_total to derive the weighted mean. Fractional abundances sum to one.'),('Solve the inverse problem.','For two isotopes, m̄ = fm₁ + (1−f)m₂. Rearranging gives f = (m₂−m̄)/(m₂−m₁). More than two unknown abundances require additional independent information; one average cannot determine an arbitrary mixture.'),('Connect the measurement to a spectrum.','A mass spectrometer ionizes and separates species by m/z. Distinct isotopes can give distinct peaks. Peak areas require suitable corrections before treating them as isotope abundances; different charge states can create additional peaks.')],
'm̄ = (ΣN_i m_i)/N_total = Σf_i m_i\nf = (m₂ − m̄)/(m₂ − m₁) for a complete two-isotope model',
'A hypothetical element has isotope masses 10.00 u and 11.00 u and mean 10.30 u. The lighter isotope fraction is (11.00−10.30)/(11.00−10.00) = 0.70, or 70%.',
