[('Distinguish pathways with the same overall pattern.','Alkene addition can proceed by different mechanisms depending on reagents and conditions. Polar curved arrows track electron pairs from a donor bond or lone pair to an acceptor site; radical arrows track single electrons. Stoichiometric atom balance alone cannot select a mechanism.'),('Connect esterification to equilibrium.','Acid catalysis changes the route of acid–alcohol esterification but not its equilibrium constant at a given temperature. Changing activities, such as by removing water under suitable conditions, can shift the attainable conversion.'),('Derive an ideal step-growth chain length.','Start with N₀ difunctional monomer molecules in a balanced ideal system. Each intermolecular bond-forming step reduces the molecule count by one. At reacted functional-group fraction p, approximately pN₀ links have formed, leaving N=N₀(1−p) chain-bearing molecules, excluding small condensate byproducts. Thus X̄_n=N₀/N=1/(1−p). High chain length requires very high conversion.')],
